al chain-growth chemistry that yields branched alcohols need not fully hydrogenate all C4 products. A direct isobutyraldehyde branch is therefore plausible and gives the shortest access to valine precursors."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Cyanohydrin capture of isobutyraldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "concentrated prebiotic pools or mineral interfaces; mild basicity converts a fraction of HCN to cyanide"}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds nucleophilically to the aldehyde carbonyl, and protonation gives the cyanohydrin.", "reasoning": "Cyanohydrin formation is a robust prebiotic way to trap reactive aldehydes and install a masked carboxylate equivalent while preserving the branched carbon skeleton required for valine."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Hydrolysis of alpha-hydroxyisovaleronitrile", "inputs": ["mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "370-410K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "subseafloor hot water with extended residence times"}, "mechanism": "The nitrile hydrolyzes stepwise through amide-like intermediates to the alpha-hydroxy acid.", "reasoning": "Nitriles are persistent enough to accumulate but hydrolyze in hot aqueous settings on geologic timescales. This step converts the cyanohydrin into a more directly redox-active hydroxy acid precursor."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Oxidation of alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid to alpha-ketoisovaleric acid", "inputs": ["mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_002"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["magnetite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "380-420K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "magnetite", "aqueous": true, "other": "redox-active iron oxide surfaces under anoxic circulation; likely a bottleneck step"}, "mechanism": "Surf