m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Valine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Center the network on isobutyraldehyde, a branched C4 hub obtainable hydrothermally either directly from CO/H2 homologation or indirectly through isobutanol. Cyanide is supplied either as ambient HCN or by surface formamide dehydration. From this hub, valine is reached by three prebiotically plausible modules: (i) Strecker aminonitrile formation followed by hydrolysis, (ii) diversion of the aminonitrile to a hydantoin reservoir followed by hydrolysis, and (iii) cyanohydrin capture followed by nitrile hydrolysis, oxidation to alpha-ketoisovaleric acid, and hydrothermal reductive amination. Ten distinct reaction sequences through these modules are enumerated below; all would furnish racemic valine.\n\nMOLECULES (14 nodes):\n  - mol_001: CO (carbon monoxide) [starting]\n  - mol_002: H2 (hydrogen) [starting]\n  - mol_003: NH3 (ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_004: HCN (hydrogen cyanide) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_005: CH3NO (formamide) [starting]\n  - mol_006: CO2 (carbon dioxide) [starting]\n  - mol_007: C4H10O (isobutanol) [intermediate]\n  - mol_008: C4H8O (isobutyraldehyde) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_009: C5H9NO (alpha-hydroxyisovaleronitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_010: C5H10O3 (alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid) [intermediate]\n  - mol_011: C5H8O3 (alpha-ketoisovaleric acid) [intermediate]\n  - mol_012: C5H10N2 (valine aminonitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_013: C6H10N2O2 (5-isopropylhydantoin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_014: C5H11NO2 (valine) [target]\n\nREACTIONS (12 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_005 -> mol_004 (Formamide dehydration to hydrogen cyanide) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_001, mol_002 -> mol_007 (Hydrothermal homologation of CO to isobutanol) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_003: mol_007 -> mol_008, mol_002 (Isobutanol dehydrogenation to isobutyraldehyde) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_004: mol_001, mol_002 -> mol_008 (Hydrothermal homologation of CO to isobutyraldehyde) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_005: mol_008, mol_004 -> mol_009 (Cyanohydrin capture of isobutyraldehyde) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_006: mol_009 -> mol_010 (Hydrolysis of alpha-hydroxyisovaleronitrile) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_007: mol_010 -> mol_011, mol_002 (Oxidation of alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid to alpha-ketoisovaleric acid) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_008: mol_011, mol_003, mol_002 -> mol_014 (Reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovaleric acid to valine) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_009: mol_008, mol_003, mol_004 -> mol_012 (Strecker synthesis of valine aminonitrile) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_010: mol_012 -> mol_014 (Hydrolysis of valine aminonitrile to valine) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_011: mol_012, mol_003, mol_006 -> mol_013 (Hydantoin formation from valine aminonitrile) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_012: mol_013 -> mol_014 (Hydrolysis of 5-isopropylhydantoin to valine) [Hydrothermal]\n\nPATHWAYS (10):\n  - P1: Direct aldehyde Strecker-hydrolysis route (3 steps)\n  - P2: Direct aldehyde hydantoin route (4 steps)\n  - P3: Formamide-assisted direct aldehyde Strecker route (4 steps)\n  - P4: Formamide-assisted direct aldehyde hydantoin route (5 steps)\n  - P5: Alcohol-mediated Strecker route (4 steps)\n  - P6: Alcohol-mediated hydantoin route (5 steps)\n  - P7: Formamide-assisted alcohol-mediated Strecker route (5 steps)\n  - P8: Direct aldehyde cyanohydrin-keto-acid route (5 steps)\n  - P9: Alcohol-mediated cyanohydrin-keto-acid route (6 steps)\n  - P10: Formamide-assisted alcohol-mediated cyanohydrin route (7 steps)\n\n\n=== DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK (RDKit) ===\nComputed programmatically from each molecule's InChI (via RDKit) or stated formula. Reactions list no coefficients, so a reaction counts as balanced if some positive integer coefficients (<= 24) conserve every element and net charge.\nSummary: 12 reactions | 3 balanced as written | 0 balanceable with coefficients | 9 UNBALANCED | 0 unchecked\n  - rxn_001 UNBALANCED: CH3NO -> HCN | reactants minus products: H+2, O+1 | balanceable if adding: H2O as product\n  - rxn_002 UNBALANCED: CO + H2 -> C4H10O | reactants minus products: C-3, H-8 | balanceable if adding: H2O as product; CO2 as product; O2 as product\n  - rxn_004 UNBALANCED: CO + H2 -> C4H8O | reactants minus products: C-3, H-6 | balanceable if adding: H2O as product; CO2 as product; O2 as product\n  - rxn_006 UNBALANCED: C5H9NO -> C5H10O3 | reactants minus products: H-1, N+1, O-2 | no single common small species (H2O, H2, NH3, CO2, ...) fixes it\n  - rxn_008 UNBALANCED: C5H8O3 + NH3 + H2 -> C5H11NO2 | reactants minus products: H+2, O+1 | balanceable if adding: H2O as product\n  - rxn_009 UNBALANCED: C4H8O + NH3 + HCN -> C5H10N2 | reactants minus products: H+2, O+1 | balanceable if adding: H2O as product; O2 as product\n  - rxn_010 UNBALANCED: C5H10N2 -> C5H11NO2 | reactants minus products: H-1, N+1, O-2 | no single common small species (H2O, H2, NH3, CO2, ...) fixes it\n  - rxn_011 UNBALANCED: C5H10N2 + NH3 + CO2 -> C6H10N2O2 | reactants minus products: H+3, N+1 | balanceable if adding: NH3 as product\n  - rxn_012 UNBALANCED: C6H10N2O2 -> C5H11NO2 | reactants minus products: C+1, H-1, N+1 | no single common small species (H2O, H2, NH3, CO2, ...) fixes it\nTreat this check as ground truth for mass and charge conservation. Reactions that cannot be balanced violate conservation: reflect this in chemical_feasibility and reaction_scores, list them in problematic_reactions, and name the specific reaction IDs (with the missing species, if hinted) in your feedback so the network can be corrected. Note that implausibly large inferred coefficients may also indicate a mis-specified reaction.\n=== END BALANCE CHECK ===\n\n\nPlease evaluate the network and respond with JSON:\n{\n    \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n        {\n            \"pathway_id\": \"<id>\",\n            \"reaction_scores\": {\"rxn_001\": 0.85, ...},\n            \"chemical_feasibility\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"pathway_coherence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"environmental_progression\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"target_achievement\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"overall_confidence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"feedback\": \"<pathway-specific feedback>\",\n            \"approved\": <true if confidence >= 0.8>,\n            \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_ids with issues\"],\n            \"suggestions\": [\"improvements\"]\n        }\n    ],\n    \"best_pathway_id\": \"<id of best pathway>\",\n    \"overall_confidence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n    \"network_feedback\": \"<overall assessment>\"\n}\n\nNote: \"environmental_progression\" scores Environmental Consistency (conditions fit each\nreaction's stated environment), not any particular order of environments.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.15,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.5,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.32\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.32,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.74,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.41,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.43,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.42,\n      \"feedback\": \"Conceptually this is the strongest route: aldehyde Strecker synthesis followed by aminonitrile hydrolysis is a valid strategy to valine. As written, however, every step in the path violates conservation: rxn_004 compresses C1