{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T23:07:31.230748", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Valine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.34\n- Feedback: The network is connected and has adequate node count, and several route motifs are chemically recognizable. The chemically strongest steps are rxn_003, rxn_005, and rxn_007. However, the deterministic balance check shows 9 of 12 reactions are unbalanced, so the network is not chemically valid as written. A H2O node is missing even though many steps need it. The required fixes are specific: rxn_001 should be formamide -> HCN + H2O; rxn_002 and rxn_004 need multicarbon CO/H2 stoichiometries and coproducts such as H2O; rxn_006 should consume 2 H2O and release NH3; rxn_008 and rxn_009 should produce H2O; rxn_010 should consume 2 H2O and release NH3; rxn_011 is mis-specified for hydantoin formation and likely needs NH3 removed from the reactant side or replacement by an explicit carbamylation or Bucherer-Bergs sequence; and rxn_012 should consume 2 H2O and release NH3 + CO2. Environmental consistency is only moderate because transport between surface, hydrothermal, and aqueous nonenzymatic settings is implicit, the label biochemical is too vague for a prebiotic environment, and catalysts or minerals are not identified for selective branched C4 formation or reductive amination. Literature support is not documented for the most speculative steps, especially branched C4 syngas homologation and hydrothermal keto-acid reductive amination. P1 is the best conceptual pathway, but no pathway is approved and the network is not approved in its current form.\n- Issues: ['Conceptually this is the strongest route: aldehyde Strecker synthesis followed by aminonitrile hydrolysis is a valid strategy to valine. As written, however, every step in the path violates conservation: rxn_004 compresses C1 homologation to a branched C4 aldehyde without the required stoichiometry or coproducts, rxn_009 should produce H2O, and rxn_010 should consume water and release NH3. The hydrothermal to aqueous nonenzymatic to hydrothermal handoff is also implicit rather than justified.', 'The outline is recognizable, but the hydantoin branch is the weakest part of the network. rxn_004 and rxn_009 are unbalanced as above; rxn_011 is not a defensible stoichiometric description of hydantoin formation from the aminonitrile; rxn_012 omits the water demand and likely NH3/CO2 release of hydantoin hydrolysis.', 'Adding formamide as an HCN source is reasonable in principle, but rxn_001 is unbalanced and should produce H2O. The core Strecker logic remains coherent, yet rxn_004, rxn_009, and rxn_010 still fail conservation, and the route now also requires an unmodeled surface-to-hydrothermal-to-aqueous transfer of reactive HCN.', 'This pathway combines the same unbalanced formamide and C4-generation steps with the underdefined hydantoin module. rxn_001 should produce H2O; rxn_004 is an underspecified homologation; rxn_009 should produce H2O; rxn_011 is not convincingly formulated; and rxn_012 omits hydrolytic water and likely NH3/CO2 byproducts.', 'Using isobutanol as a precursor to isobutyraldehyde is chemically sensible because rxn_003 is balanced and plausible with an appropriate catalyst. The limiting issue is rxn_002: a single CO + H2 -> isobutanol node is far too compressed and unbalanced. The downstream Strecker module also still needs H2O/NH3 bookkeeping in rxn_009 and rxn_010.', 'The alcohol-to-aldehyde entry is acceptable in principle because rxn_003 is balanced, but the pathway is undermined by the unbalanced and highly compressed syngas-to-alcohol step rxn_002 and by the poorly specified hydantoin chemistry. rxn_009 should produce H2O, rxn_011 is not well justified as written, and rxn_012 omits required hydrolysis stoichiometry.', 'This route adds a plausible HCN source and a balanced alcohol dehydrogenation step, but it still depends on two unbalanced entry reactions: rxn_001 should produce H2O and rxn_002 is an overcompressed syngas homologation. The Strecker module remains only partly specified because rxn_009 should produce H2O and rxn_010 should consume water and release NH3. Multiple surface, hydrothermal, and aqueous transfers are implicit.', 'This pathway has coherent carbon-skeleton logic: aldehyde -> cyanohydrin -> hydroxy acid -> keto acid -> valine. rxn_005 and rxn_007 are balanced and reasonable, but rxn_004 is still an unbalanced, highly compressed C4 formation step, rxn_006 omits 2 H2O consumption and NH3 release, and rxn_008 omits H2O and assumes hydrothermal reductive amination with H2 without identifying a realistic catalyst.', 'The middle of this pathway is stronger than its entry and exit: rxn_003, rxn_005, and rxn_007 are plausible, but rxn_002 is an unbalanced and overly compressed syngas-to-isobutanol step. rxn_006 should consume water and release NH3, while rxn_008 needs H2O in the stoichiometry and better justification for direct hydrothermal reductive amination.', 'This is the most environment-intensive route and suffers from cumulative specification problems. rxn_001 should produce H2O, rxn_002 is an unbalanced and highly compressed syngas homologation, rxn_006 omits water and NH3, and rxn_008 omits H2O and lacks a clear catalytic basis. The internally balanced steps rxn_003, rxn_005, and rxn_007 help, but they do not rescue the pathway as written.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Valine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Valine is reached by first building the branched C4 Strecker precursor isobutyraldehyde through two successive C1 aldol/dehydration homologations: acetaldehyde + formaldehyde -> acrolein -> propanal, then propanal + formaldehyde -> methacrolein -> isobutyraldehyde. Simple aldehydes can be taken directly from the wider prebiotic inventory or generated in-network from CO/H2, and HCN can be taken directly or released from formamide. A final balanced Strecker step followed by explicit aminonitrile hydrolysis furnishes valine. The network deliberately avoids a one-step branched C4 syngas reaction and keeps all water and ammonia coproducts explicit.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon monoxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon monoxide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH3NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"prop-2-enal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acrolein\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"propanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"propionaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H6O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-methylprop-2-enal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"methacrolein\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-methylpropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H10N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"valine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H11NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-methylbutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"valine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal CO hydrogenation to formaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"awaruite\", \"magnetite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-430K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"awaruite/magnetite\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"anoxic H2-rich subseafloor or alkaline vent setting\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"surface-mediated reduction of CO to a C1 oxygenate on reduced Fe-Ni mineral phases\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"H2-rich hydrothermal systems containing reduced Fe-Ni minerals can reduce CO to small oxygenates without invoking O2. Formaldehyde is unlikely to accumulate at the vent itself, but even low yields make it a plausible feeder to later surface chemistry and a justified in-network source alongside exogenous atmospheric formaldehyde.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal syngas coupling to acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe-Ni alloy surfaces\", \"greigite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"400-500K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe-Ni alloy/greigite\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"anoxic H2-rich vent fluid; Fischer-Tropsch-type regime\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"net CO reduction with C-C coupling and aldehyde termination\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Compared with a direct branched C4 synthesis, linear C2 aldehyde formation from CO/H2 is the chemically safer Fischer-Tropsch-type option. Selectivity would be low and product mixtures are expected, but acetaldehyde is a plausible minor product; water is included explicitly as coproduct.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Formamide dehydration to hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"volcanic silica\", \"alumina\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"380-470K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"silica/alumina surfaces\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"false\",\n        \"other\": \"dry-down films at volcanic hot springs or impact-heated mineral surfaces\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"thermal dehydration of formamide under drying conditions\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Formamide thermolysis or dehydration in drying volcanic or impact-heated settings gives HCN and water. This provides a balanced cyanide source and avoids omitting the obligatory water coproduct.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Aldol condensation of acetaldehyde and formaldehyde to acrolein\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite\", \"carbonate minerals\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"clay/carbonate mineral surfaces\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"wet-dry cycling in evaporitic pools; dehydration favored during dry phase\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"acetaldehyde enolization, C-C bond formation with formaldehyde, then dehydration\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Surface concentration is important because the step is a net aldol condensation plus dehydration. Acetaldehyde is the nucleophilic partner, formaldehyde provides one-carbon homologation, and acrolein is treated as a transient enal en route to the saturated aldehyde.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogenation of acrolein to propanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni-Fe sulfides\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-380K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni-Fe sulfides\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"false\",\n        \"other\": \"reducing geothermal gas-mineral interface with H2 available\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"mineral-catalyzed reduction of the enal C=C bond while retaining the aldehyde\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Reducing geothermal surfaces can supply both H2 and catalytic metal sulfides. Chemoselectivity will not be perfect, but hydrogenation of the enal C=C bond to propanal is chemically reasonable and preserves the aldehyde needed for the second homologation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Aldol condensation of propanal and formaldehyde to methacrolein\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite\", \"carbonate minerals\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"clay/carbonate mineral surfaces\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"evaporative concentration; dehydration favored during dry intervals\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"propanal enolization, C-C bond formation with formaldehyde, then dehydration\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"A second formaldehyde aldol/dehydration introduces the branch corresponding to the valine side chain. Placing this step at the surface is justified because concentration and water loss are required; methacrolein is again treated as a short-lived intermediate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogenation of methacrolein to isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni-Fe sulfides\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-380K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni-Fe sulfides\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"false\",\n        \"other\": \"reducing geothermal gas-mineral interface with H2 available\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"mineral-catalyzed reduction of the alkene in the branched enal\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Reduction of methacrolein to isobutyraldehyde gives the canonical branched aldehyde precursor to valine and avoids the much weaker assumption of direct branched C4 synthesis from syngas.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker synthesis of valine aminonitrile from isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"cyanide- and ammonia-bearing evaporitic pool or geothermal pond\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"imine formation with ammonia, cyanide addition, and aminonitrile capture\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The Strecker reaction from isobutyraldehyde is the strongest prebiotic entry to valine. Water is shown explicitly as coproduct, and the step can draw HCN either from the broader environment or from rxn_003.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of valine aminonitrile to valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"prebiotic pool or mineral interface under geothermal heating-cooling cycles\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"stepwise nitrile hydration to amide and acid under prolonged aqueous heating\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aminonitrile hydrolysis is slow but geochemically plausible during repeated hydration and heating. Two waters are required stoichiometrically and ammonia is released explicitly, giving a fully balanced conversion to valine.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Fully in-network feedstock generation\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde and acetaldehyde arise from hydrothermal CO/H2 chemistry, HCN arises from formamide dehydration, and the branched aldehyde is built by two surface aldol/hydrogenation cycles before Strecker conversion to valine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"In-network carbonyls with direct environmental HCN\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal CO/H2 supplies formaldehyde and acetaldehyde, but cyanide is assumed to be available directly from the wider surface inventory before Strecker conversion.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct formaldehyde entry with generated HCN\",\n      \"description\": \"Exogenous formaldehyde is combined with hydrothermally generated acetaldehyde, while formamide supplies cyanide for the final Strecker step.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct formaldehyde entry with direct HCN\",\n      \"description\": \"Only acetaldehyde is generated in-network; formaldehyde and HCN are taken directly from the prebiotic inventory and then passed through the same branched-aldehyde/Strecker sequence.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct acetaldehyde entry with generated HCN\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal CO/H2 supplies formaldehyde, acetaldehyde is assumed directly available, and