dehyde is a plausible minor product; water is included explicitly as coproduct."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Formamide dehydration to hydrogen cyanide", "inputs": ["mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["volcanic silica", "alumina"], "conditions": {"temperature": "380-470K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "silica/alumina surfaces", "aqueous": "false", "other": "dry-down films at volcanic hot springs or impact-heated mineral surfaces"}, "mechanism": "thermal dehydration of formamide under drying conditions", "reasoning": "Formamide thermolysis or dehydration in drying volcanic or impact-heated settings gives HCN and water. This provides a balanced cyanide source and avoids omitting the obligatory water coproduct."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Aldol condensation of acetaldehyde and formaldehyde to acrolein", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["montmorillonite", "carbonate minerals"], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-360K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "clay/carbonate mineral surfaces", "aqueous": "true", "other": "wet-dry cycling in evaporitic pools; dehydration favored during dry phase"}, "mechanism": "acetaldehyde enolization, C-C bond formation with formaldehyde, then dehydration", "reasoning": "Surface concentration is important because the step is a net aldol condensation plus dehydration. Acetaldehyde is the nucleophilic partner, formaldehyde provides one-carbon homologation, and acrolein is treated as a transient enal en route to the saturated aldehyde."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Hydrogenation of acrolein to propanal", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Ni-Fe sulfides"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-380K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Ni-Fe sulfides", "aqueous": "false", "other": "reducing geothermal gas-mineral interface with H2 available"}, "mechanism": "mineral-catalyzed reduction of the enal C=C bond while retaining the aldehyde", "reasoning": "Reducing geothermal surfaces can supply both H2 and catalytic metal sulfides. Chemoselectivity will not be perfect, but hydrogenation of the enal C=C bond to propanal is chemically reasonable and preserves the aldehyde needed for the second homologation."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Aldol condensation of propanal and formaldehyde to methacrolein", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["montmorillonite", "carbonate minerals"], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-360K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "clay/carbonate mineral surfaces", "aqueous": "true", "other": "evaporative concentration; dehydration favored during dry intervals"}, "mechanism": "propanal enolization, C-C bond formation with formaldehyde, then dehydration", "reasoning": "A second formaldehyde aldol/dehydration introduces the branch corresponding to the valine side chain. Placing this step at the surface is justified because concentration and water loss are required; methacrolein is again treated as a short-lived intermediate."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Hydrogenation of methacrolein to isobutyraldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Ni-Fe sulfides"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-380K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Ni-Fe sulfides", "aqueous": "false", "other": "reducing geothermal gas-mineral interface with H2 available"}, "mechanism": "mineral-catalyzed reduction of the alkene in the branched enal", "reasoning": "Reduction of methacrolein to isobutyraldehyde gives the canonical branched aldehyde precursor to valine and avoids the much weaker assumption of direct branched C4 synthesis from syngas."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Strecker synthesis of valine aminonitrile from isobutyraldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_006", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_003"], 