h"}, "mechanism": "Catalytic hydrogenation of the C=C bond of methacrolein yields isobutyraldehyde.", "reasoning": "Hydrogenation converts the branched enal into isobutyraldehyde, the immediate Strecker substrate for valine. This is a second selectivity-sensitive redox step; exact prebiotic selectivity is uncertain, but the underlying chemistry is straightforward on reduced metal-bearing surfaces."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Strecker synthesis of valine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "290-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "cyanide- and ammonia-rich evaporitic pool; mild concentration by drying"}, "mechanism": "The aldehyde forms an imine or iminium with ammonia, followed by cyanide addition to give the aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "The Strecker reaction is the strongest abiotic route from an aldehyde to an alpha-amino acid precursor. Using isobutyraldehyde specifically yields valine aminonitrile in a balanced single-step transformation."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Abiotic hydrolysis of valine aminonitrile to valine", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "warm aqueous pool; prolonged hydrolysis; stoichiometrically consumes two waters"}, "mechanism": "Stepwise nitrile hydration and hydrolysis convert the aminonitrile to the amino acid, releasing ammonia.", "reasoning": "Warm abiotic aqueous hydrolysis converts the aminonitrile to valine and explicitly returns NH3. This is likely one of the slow bottlenecks of the network; two waters are required stoichiometrically, and the setting is kept explicitly prebiotic surface aqueous rather than a vague biochemical environment."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Dire